 "report": "Structure found: a trefoil knot (a closed 1-D curve, knotted) living in 3 hidden coordinates. Each coordinate is an integer combination of 5 columns taken mod 1 (15 disjoint columns in total; the other 15 columns x1,x5,x7,x9,x11,x14,x15,x17,x18,x20,x21,x23,x25,x26,x28 showed no structure in any scan and look like pure noise): u1 = mod(x3 - x6 + 2*x8 - 2*x10 - x29, 1), u2 = mod(x4 - 2*x13 - x19 + 2*x22 + x30, 1), u3 = mod(x2 + 2*x12 + x16 + x24 + 2*x27, 1). In (u1,u2,u3) all 8000 rows lie within ~0.02 of a single closed trefoil-knot curve centred near (0.526,0.528,0.527), spanning u1 in [0.42,0.63], u2 in [0.31,0.73], u3 in [0.33,0.76]; it does not cross the mod-1 border, so it is drawn as one closed loop of 200 points. Scatter around the curve is roughly isotropic with std ~0.004 per axis (mean distance 0.006); the whole loop is populated. Every individual column is uniform on [0,1] and any set of fewer than 5 of these columns is independent, which is why histograms, correlations, PCA, 2D/3D histograms and low-order Fourier scans showed nothing. How found: after those scans came back null, I looked for integer vectors a with a.x nearly constant mod 1 using lattice reduction (LLL/BKZ on row differences), which revealed u1; a GPU scan of all 5-column characters exp(2*pi*i*a.x) with coefficients in {-2..2} then gave exactly three significant combinations (statistics 6941, 4590, 4219 versus a noise maximum of ~18). The curve was fitted with a closed Fourier series (6 harmonics) and validated on held-out rows: held-out mean distance to the curve 0.0063 versus 0.32 for uniform-noise rows; only 0.2% of noise rows fall within the 99th-percentile data distance."
